  [38;5;153mSession.getUserId()      -> string | null[39m
  [38;5;153mSession.setUserId(id)    -> void[39m
  [38;5;153mSession.clearUserId()    -> void[39m

  Backed by [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m under a single namespaced key ([38;5;153mapp.userId[39m). It's an IIFE so the storage key and the fallback
  state stay private — matching the file-scope-function style already in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, with no build step and no
  [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m.

  The interface is deliberately three functions rather than a general key-value session bag. You have one value today; a
  wider interface would be guessing at what the later forms need, and widening later is cheap while narrowing is not.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m — one line added: [38;5;153m<script src="session.js"></script>[39m before the existing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m tag. Ordering matters and a
  classic script guarantees it.

  [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps its exact signature, [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m. This is the part that contradicts the literal
  request, so to be explicit: it gains no [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter. The caller has no userId to give it; the userId is what
  login [3mproduces[0m. What changes inside is that the stubbed response now carries a [38;5;153muserId[39m, and login persists it.

  [1mSection 2 — Data flow[0m

  1. Submit handler reads username/password, runs [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m (unchanged).
  2. [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m runs. Its stub response gains a [38;5;153muserId[39m field, clearly marked as standing in for a
     server-issued ID and derived deterministically from the username so tests aren't random.
  3. On success, [38;5;153mlogin()[39m calls [38;5;153mSession.setUserId(response.userId)[39m.
  4. On failure, [38;5;153mlogin()[39m calls [38;5;153mSession.clearUserId()[39m — a failed login must not leave the previous user's ID readable by
     the next form. That's why [38;5;153mclearUserId[39m is in the interface despite the app having no logout button yet.
  5. Later forms call [38;5;153mSession.getUserId()[39m after [38;5;153msession.js[39m loads. They don't know storage exists.

  The return value keeps [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m and adds [38;5;153muserId[39m, so nothing reading it today breaks. When [38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes
  async and really calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, it returns a promise of that same shape and step 3 moves inside the [38;5;153m.then[39m —
  callers change once, at that point, not now.

  Does that look right so far? Error handling and the testing/tooling question are next.

